## CI

The pipeline is a plain shell script, `scripts/ci.sh`, and runs the same way locally and in any
CI system (needs python3 for tests, Docker and git to ship):

```sh
scripts/ci.sh workloads         # list discovered workloads
scripts/ci.sh test              # run every workload's tests
scripts/ci.sh build NAME        # build one image, tagged :local
REGISTRY_USERNAME=... REGISTRY_PASSWORD=... GITOPS_TOKEN=... scripts/ci.sh ship
```

`ship` pushes `{{ project.registry }}/{{ project.name }}-<workload>:<commit>` for each workload,
then commits `environments/{{ defaults.RELEASE_ENVIRONMENT }}/<workload>/release.yaml` to
[{{ plan.gitops.folder }}]({{ plan.gitops.web_url }}). Argo CD deploys from there. Releases run one at a
time and retry if another repository pushes to the GitOps repo first.

{% if project.git_provider.value == "github" %}
`.github/workflows/ci.yaml` runs the tests on pull requests and pushes, and `ship` on pushes to `main`.
{% elif project.git_provider.value == "gitlab" %}
`.gitlab-ci.yml` runs the tests on merge requests and `ship` on pushes to the default branch.
The ship job uses Docker-in-Docker, so the runner must allow privileged containers
(GitLab.com's shared runners do).
{% else %}
No CI provider file was generated. Run the commands above from your CI system with the
settings above as environment variables.
{% endif %}

## Template updates

The `{{ defaults.TEMPLATE_BRANCH }}` branch holds plinth's untouched output. `plinth update` (run from the
folder holding this repository and {{ plan.gitops.folder }}) renders the latest templates onto that
branch and merges it here, so your own changes are kept. Push the branch along with `main`.
